## The short answer

Perfection loses its **decision layer** and keeps its **mechanism layer** almost intact.

Nearly everything that adjudicates *whether to ask about this particular parameter* becomes dead weight, because the two-line rule decides it globally. Everything that *implements* asking, gating, compiling, and recording survives — and some of it has to get stronger, because it now carries load the questions used to carry.

That also changes what kind of document Perfection is. Right now it reads as a policy manual that re-litigates ask-vs-gate locally in every domain section. Under the rule it becomes a compiler spec with a **fixed front end**: a finite shape inventory, a slot table, and a ledger recording which is which.

---

## The new architecture

**Three layers, one classifier.**

| Layer | Contains | Populated by | Bounded? |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Shape inventory** | What sections, passes, workflow steps, rubric dimensions, tiers, and branches exist | Asking | **Yes — enumerable** |
| **Slot table** | Every named parameter those shapes consume | Gating (`ON_MISSING`) | No — and that's fine |
| **Ledger** | Which shape choices came from the user, which from Goal, which from default | Compilation | Mirrors layer 1 |

The classifier is a single question asked once per unknown: *does resolving this change what the artifact contains, or only what a named slot holds?* Contains → layer 1. Holds → layer 2.

The reason the interview terminates is that layer 1 is finite and knowable in advance. You can write the inventory down before ever meeting a user. That is the structural termination I claimed last turn, and it's the whole payoff.

**One correction to the slogan before we build on it.** "Ask for structure, gate for values" compresses the three-test rule and drops two tests. Reachability moves into gate design, where it belongs. But **abstractability has to stay as a veto on asking** — a structural question the user cannot answer without a case in hand must still not be asked. Otherwise the rule licenses asking about every branch that could exist. The slogan is the routing rule; abstractability is the guard.

---

## Sections that become simpler

**§3.1 — the interview loop.** From an open-ended completeness crawl to a traversal of a finite list. No turn cap needed, because termination is a property of the inventory rather than a budget. This is the section that shrinks most in proportion to its current size.

**§6 and §7 — the domain sections.** These currently each carry a local copy of the same machinery: an importance classification, an ask-or-gate disjunction, a default policy, and in §7.6's case nested scope rules. Under the rule they retain only *content* — what shapes exist in the reviewer domain, what shapes exist in the sourcing domain — and delegate every decision upward. §7.6 collapses to its default line. §6.3 stops being a blocking item and becomes an ordinary scoped gate.

**Fast/slow mode.** Not two behaviours anymore. One traversal with a scope switch: fast covers the inventory items that gate the artifact's skeleton, slow covers the whole inventory. Same loop, same stopping condition.

**§16.3 — the robustness disclaimer.** One line, once, and moved out of the artifact header where it competes with the gates and ledger rows that are the actual product.

---

## Sections that become unnecessary

**The Tier 1 / Tier 2 system.** This is the big casualty and worth being explicit about why. Tiering is an *importance* ranking being used as a proxy for *ask-worthiness*. Once ask-worthiness is determined by shape-vs-slot, importance no longer selects the mechanism. It doesn't vanish — it relocates into gate design, as `ON_MISSING` severity: halt, default-and-flag, or proceed. That's where it was always doing real work.

**Every "question it, or gate it" disjunction.** The disjunction *is* the creep engine. Discretion at the clause level, exercised under a cost model that prices questions at zero, resolves toward asking every time. Removing the choice removes the drift without needing to police it.

**The over-classification cost model.** Already replaced.

**The checkpoint's gag clause and its repeat behaviour.** It fires once, reports a satisfied objective, recommends compiling.

**§15.1's re-interview mandate.** Mostly obsolete: the values it re-asks about are now gates, and the structural choices are covered by the narrowed provenance contract.

**Not on this list: §9.** No-invention stays, untouched, as a constraint outside the tradeoff.

---

## Sections that become more important

This rule does not reduce total design work. It relocates it — and I'd rather name where the risk lands than sell the simplification.

**§4 / the gate mechanism becomes the primary product surface.** Gate volume rises sharply, and gates now carry what questions used to. That raises three requirements it may not currently meet: unresolved gates must be *visible* rather than buried; halt-vs-default semantics must be per-gate and explicit; and gate scoping needs to work when one gate's shape depends on another's resolution.

**The shape inventory becomes new load-bearing content that doesn't exist yet.** The rule is exactly as good as the enumeration. Anything structural you fail to list is something you will silently gate — which means silently defaulting or silently halting. This is the single largest new risk in the architecture and the thing I'd build first, because it's also the thing that makes the termination claim true or false.

**The structural/value boundary needs a tie-breaker.** We already have two cases that look alike and route differently: *how many source tiers exist* is structure, *the ordering over a named set of tiers* is a value. That distinction is defensible but not self-evident, and without a stated tie-breaker every clause revision will re-argue it. My candidate: if the answer changes the artifact's *text*, it's structure; if it changes only a *binding*, it's a value.

**The ledger becomes the honesty mechanism.** It's now the only record distinguishing user choices from Goal's inferences from defaults. It absorbs the burden §16.3's verbatim caveats were carrying badly.

**Pass B and the rubric section define the inventory.** They're the sections whose shape depends most on answers, so they effectively specify what the interview must cover. Worth writing them first for that reason alone.

---

## One gap in the two-line rule

There's a third case it doesn't route: a value the user cannot supply **at runtime either** — no rubric exists, no reviewer exists, the information isn't held anywhere. Gating that is a guaranteed halt dressed up as deferral, which is worse than either branch, because it looks handled.

That needs a third destination: **declare out of scope in the ledger**, and compile an artifact that doesn't depend on it. Otherwise the rule quietly converts "we don't know" into "the run fails later," which is precisely the reliability failure the objective was chosen to prevent.

I'd want that branch settled before touching individual clauses, since it determines whether the slot table is allowed to contain items with no possible source.
